outlook is grim. Many insects sit in this limbo. ### 3. Why Insects (and Other Small Creatures) Are a Special Nightmare For small or inconspicuous organisms, the challenges multiply: - **Sheer number & under-description:** Over 80% of insect species are thought to be undescribed. We can’t declare something extinct if we never knew it existed. - **Tiny size & cryptic habits:** Many insects hide in leaf litter, soil, deep canopy, or inside other organisms. Some are only active for a few weeks a year and only under specific conditions (e.g., a fungus gnat that emerges only after a certain rainfall). - **Sampling bias:** Collecting methods (malaise traps, light traps, pitfall traps) are selective. A species might simply not be attracted to the method we’re using. Even intensive hand-searching can miss microhabitats. - **Spectacular “Lazarus” rediscoveries:** The insect world is full of them. The **Lord Howe Island stick insect** was thought extinct for 80 years (declared extinct in the 1960s) until a tiny population was found on a sea stack. The **blue calamintha bee** (*Osmia calaminthae*) was rediscovered in Florida in 2020 after several years with no sightings. These stories underscore how easily a small population can persist in a micro-refuge. - **Lack of funding and expertise:** There are far fewer entomologists than birdwatchers. For many rare insects, the last person who could reliably identify them might have retired. The survey effort is often just a fraction of what it takes to be truly exhaustive. ### 4. Tools That Improve (But Don’t Perfect) Verification Modern techniques reduce the guesswork: - **Environmental DNA (eDNA):** Sampling water, soil, or air for trace DNA can detect a species without ever seeing an individual. It’s been used to rediscover amphibians and fish, but for terrestrial insects in soil or leaf litter, it’s still developing and far from foolproof (DNA degrades, reference databases are incomplete). - **Automated monitoring:** Acoustic sensors, camera traps with AI, and malaise trap samples sorted by DNA metabarcoding can process vast amounts of material across seasons and years, increasing the odds of detection. - **Citizen science:** Platforms like iNaturalist have accidentally documented insects that experts hadn’t seen in decades, expanding the effective survey net. Even so, these tools can only tell us if something **is** there. A negative result (no DNA, no photo) still isn’t proof of absence. ### Bottom line A species is **determined** extinct when the balance of evidence—quality, duration, and scope of searches—makes survival so improbable that expert bodies accept it. For insects, this is particularly shaky ground. In truth, “extinction” is a label applied with a degree of humility: it’s the best hypothesis we have until someone brings a living specimen back to the lab. Many insect extinctions are likely occurring silently, never to be verified. And on the flip side, some declared extinct may still persist in a single rotting log that nobody has looked inside yet.
